<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Xin (Western Liang)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/420">420</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Xin was the second ruler of Western Liang, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, reigning from 417 to 420 as the son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a>. Against advice, he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> of Northern Liang and was killed in battle in 420. Northern Liang then took Jiuquan, and Western Liang fell the following year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Xin, courtesy name Shiye, was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a>, who founded Western Liang in 400 after declaring independence from Northern Liang. His birth date is unknown, as is whether his mother, Lady Yin, was Li Gao's wife or concubine. He became heir apparent in 404 on the death of his elder brother Li Tan. In 410 he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> of Northern Liang, and his father had to ransom the captured general Zhu Yuanhu with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>; in 411 he defeated Juqu Mengxun and captured his general Juqu Bainian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his deathbed in 417 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a> entrusted Li Xin to his half-brother Song Yao, whom Li Xin made Libationer of the Military Council, a post close to chief minister. Li Xin's harsh punishments and palace building burdened his people. Later in 417 he saw through a feigned surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a> arranged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a>, withdrew and defeated his pursuer. In 418, on the advice of his official Zhang Tishun, he stayed in his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> when Juqu Mengxun attacked, and the Jin court, to which he had offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, made him Duke of Jiuquan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 419 his officials Zhang Xian and Fan Cheng urged him to be more lenient and thrifty, but he ignored them. In 420 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> feigned a campaign against Haomen in Western Qin, then hid his army at Chuanyan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a>. Against the advice of Song Yao, Zhang Tishun and his mother, Li Xin attacked Zhangye and was defeated. Refusing to withdraw, saying he could face his mother again only after a victory, he fought once more and was killed. His son Li Zhong'er later served Liu Song and Northern Wei; the Tang emperors descended from him through Li Yuan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Xin (Western Liang)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Li Gao's son <b>Li Xin</b> was killed in battle with Northern Liang in 420; Juqu Mengxun then took Jiuquan, and in 421 he flooded Dunhuang and killed Li Xun.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-liang">Western Liang</a></li></ul></section>
